A||N||10||cowans||GT Seat Speakers???||||1||
Z||000000||cowans||12-21-2000||08:30 AM||||What are the best replacement speakers for the GT seats the people have used? & Who sells them?? thanks, Sandy||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||12-21-2000||10:06 AM||||Assuming you mean 1985 GT speakers in the seats, Many companies sell a 3 1/2" speaker. You will need 2 sets (1 set per seat)<BR>Polk Audio and Infinity sound the best (to me). In an 84 a friend put in the Polk Audio's. He got them at "Best Buy" for around $39.00 a set.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||12-21-2000||10:41 AM||||You can't go wrong with the 3-1/2" Infinity or Polk speakers like Oreif says. I've had a pair of 3-1/2" Kicker Impulse coaxials in the dash of my Suburban for several years and I'm quite pleased with them. JBL is another fine brand. I recommend using the right size "bass blockers" with small speakers like 3-1/2 inchers.||||reg||1
Z||000003||pHoOl||12-21-2000||08:27 PM||||Will four inchers fit in there?  Also, can anyone post a pic of how you put them in?  I have an 88, so i've always wanted to know.<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||12-21-2000||09:09 PM||||'88s don't have speaker seats.  Only the '84s and '85s.  After that Pontiac got smart and moved the speakers to the rear fill panel.  (Better choice of speakers, easier installation, better sound, etc.)<P>You have to remove the seat back cover to get to the speakers.  The stock speakers just sit in the foam if I remember correctly.  They drive me nuts, though.  Unless you're about 5 ft tall, they drive into your shoulders instead of your ears.||||reg||1
Z||000005||KRMFiero||12-21-2000||09:41 PM||||I have AudioBahn 3 1/2's in my seats... I think they sound really good<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<BR>Front - AudioBahn 4 x 10<BR>Headrest - AudioBahn 3 1/2<P> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||pHoOl||12-21-2000||09:50 PM||||i know, i was thinking of adding them if i end up getting new seats or just put them in the ones that i have.  <P>Good point though about the five feet tall thing.  Never thought of that.  i just think it's cool that they put speakers in the head rests. did anybody else do this?  fords or ferraris or anything?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||cowans||12-22-2000||08:22 AM||||Thanks to all of you who responded. Some good advice...What a great forum! Merry Christmas to all!!! Sandy||||reg||7
Z||000008||batboy||12-22-2000||10:03 AM||||Well, I'm not very tall, but significantly taller than 5', and those headrest speakers never have poked me. I would think since they are mounted in foam, you ought to be able to install 4" speakers with no problem (maybe with only minor trimming). It's amazing how much better sound you can get out of 4" compared to 3-1/2" drivers. Definitely go with a good brand of coaxials.||||reg||1
Z||000009||BAM-BAM||12-22-2000||11:33 AM||||  pHoOL, Here's a link a the place that will explain the proccess of adding the speakers or replacing them, complete with pictures. <A HREF="http://www.mrmikes.com" TARGET=_blank>www.mrmikes.com</A>  look under the Frequently Asked Questions heading. Can't go wrong. Also, in the same price range you will find myriad speakers by myriad manufacturers that are compatable. However, do not doscount the experiecnce of some of the above posted people, they be real smart guys!. I personaly am leaning toward the JBL's, only because they have the rest of the speakers that I want to use.<P>  Peace, B2 OUT!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH<P>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 12-22-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 12-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||12-22-2000||11:46 AM||||BAM-BAM has another good point. When replacing speakers, (if you are replacing them all,) try to stay with the same brand. The reason is different manufacturers set the crossover points at different freqs. If you get a set of speakers (co-axial) that sets the crossover at 5000hz and another set crossed at 4000hz, you may mess up the staging and it may sound slightly out of balance. Most decent/well known brands list what the crossover point is so you can match them up. Just something to watch for when setting up sound in a car. ||||reg||1
